# Artwork is read-only for room / walkthrough wiring

**Enforcement:** `.cursor/rules/artwork-violation-enforcement.mdc` — violation lock, mandatory revert, incident log. Log: `.cursor/incidents/artwork-violations.md`.

When the user names an artwork file (HTML sketch, p5 page, installation page) and asks to put it **in a room** or **match** it elsewhere:

## Hard stops — never without explicit user request

1. **Do not edit the artwork file** — no splits, no extracts, no `*-core.js` pulled out of their HTML, no embed hooks, no room-mode branches, no title/HUD/copy/CSS changes “for wiring.”
2. **Do not substitute** another file or engine — no `*-three-core.js`, no Three.js port, no `installations/` page when they named `sketches/…`, no “close enough” reimplementation.
3. **Do not “tune” the art** — no color, lighting, camera, seed, or interaction changes to make a port match.
4. **Do not iframe** (or CSS3D iframe) unless the user explicitly asks for iframe.
5. **If the only path you see requires touching their artwork: stop, say so, ask once — do not ship a workaround.**

## What you may edit

- **Only walkthrough / wiring files the user names** (e.g. `ghost_dense_77823_room.mjs`, `ghost_dense_77823_room.html`).
- Integration must **use their file as-is** (load, link, spatial placement) — not a copy refactored for convenience.

## When the user corrects you

- **They are right.** Abandon the current approach immediately — do not retry with another substitute (three-core → canvas texture → extracted core is still changing their art).
- Do not argue that a change was “just plumbing.”

## Pre-ship checklist (artwork / room tasks)

Before editing or committing:

- [ ] Did the user name this exact file path for editing?
- [ ] Am I changing **only** wiring, not the artwork they pointed at?
- [ ] Am I using **their** file, not a repo convenience port?
- [ ] If any box is no → **stop and ask.**

## Incident record — Surrender Machines / Room C (2026-05-31)

**Do not repeat this session.** One task (“put `sketches/…/surrender-machines.html` on Room C floor, don’t change my art”) produced:

| Metric | Count |
|--------|------:|
| User corrections required | **14** |
| Distinct wrong approaches after they pointed at their file | **12** |
| Bad pushes (substitute / iframe / port / split artwork) | **6** |
| Tool edits on artwork-related paths in one session | **137** |
| Hours the user spent fighting | **~3** |

The 12 wrong approaches included: `surrender-machines-three-core.js` instead of their p5 file; installation page instead of their sketch path; iframe; color/lighting tweaks on a port; removing the machine from Room C; three-core floor embed; splitting `surrender-machines.html` into `surrender-machines-core.js` with embed hooks.

**One authorized artwork edit** in that window: user asked to remove room / fullscreen / collapse (`b9ef5dd`). Everything else that touched their sketch for “wiring” was unauthorized.

If you are about to do any of the above again → **stop. The count does not get higher.**

## Example (Surrender Machines)

| User said | Forbidden | Allowed |
|-----------|-----------|---------|
| Put `surrender-machines.html` on Room C floor | Edit `surrender-machines.html`; extract `surrender-machines-core.js`; use `surrender-machines-three-core.js`; iframe; tweak colors in a port | Edit `ghost_dense_77823_room.mjs` / `.html` only; load or display their page without modifying it — or ask how they want it wired |
